language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ session2_subset_sum: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ A bank van carried several bags of coins. Each bag holds one of the given numbers of coins, and there are several bags of each kind. Thieves stole some of the bags, and a given total number of coins was lost. Find how many bags were stolen of each type of coin bag.
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `session2_subset_sum.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/session2_subset_sum.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `session2_subset_sum_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py session2_subset_sum.json
$   savilerow model.eprime session2_subset_sum_1.param -run-solver

$ total_coins_lost: the total number of coins stolen
given total_coins_lost : int(0..)
$ coin_numbers_dim1: the number of bag types (derived by the converter from the data)
given coin_numbers_dim1 : int(1..)
$ coin_numbers[i]: number of coins in a bag of type i (indices 1-based)
given coin_numbers : matrix indexed by [int(1..coin_numbers_dim1)] of int(1..)

$ bags[i]: number of bags of type i that were stolen
find bags : matrix indexed by [int(1..coin_numbers_dim1)] of int(0..total_coins_lost)

$ search hint: branch on the bag counts first (speeds up the search)
branching on [bags]

such that
$ the stolen bags of all types together hold exactly the total number of coins lost
(sum i : int(1..coin_numbers_dim1) . bags[i] * coin_numbers[i]) = total_coins_lost
